/* Deep lessons — Advanced algorithms (advanced-cp).
 *
 * This is the last track, and it is the one where a learner most often stalls
 * for the wrong reason. The techniques here are not harder to *write* than a
 * segment tree; they are harder to RECOGNISE. Nobody fails FFT because the
 * butterfly loop is difficult — they fail it because they never noticed the
 * problem was asking for a convolution. Nobody fails Mo's algorithm on the
 * comparator; they fail it because they never saw that the queries could be
 * answered offline, in any order they liked.
 *
 * So every stage here answers three questions in this order:
 *
 *   1. WHICH SHAPE OF PROBLEM does this tool own — the recognition signal
 *   2. WHY it works — the one invariant or identity everything rests on
 *   3. HOW it is written, line by line, with the traps marked
 *
 * The code is contest C++ and is meant to be read, retyped and kept. Where a
 * shorter-but-fragile version is popular, the lesson says so out loud.
 *
 * Original text and diagrams. Nothing copied from any source.
 */

